# Wick — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely. Choose the types, the
fields, and the modules that hold them to suit the implementation you write.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors
what they write, and returns to its title-screen values when the surface's
`reset` is called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is
yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following, because the rest of this
specification is written against them:

- The screen the game is showing, one of `title`, `howto`, `playing`,
  `levelup`, `chest`, `paused`, `fallen`, or `dawn`, and the highlighted index
  of whichever vertical menu that screen shows.
- The run clock, as a whole count of ticks since the run started at `TICK_HZ`
  (`60`) ticks per second, and the frame-time remainder waiting for the next
  whole tick, which grows on `playing` alone: the delta time left unconsumed
  when the screen leaves `playing` is discarded, so the remainder is `0` on
  every other screen.
- The lamplighter: its world position, its facing (`left` or `right`), and its
  health. Maximum health, armor, move speed, recovery, and pickup radius are
  derived from the held passives rather than stored.
- The loadout: up to `WEAPON_SLOTS` (`6`) weapons, each with its id, its level,
  and the seconds until it next fires, in slot order; and up to `PASSIVE_SLOTS`
  (`6`) passives, each with its id and its level, in slot order.
- Progression: the level, the experience toward the next level, the kill
  count, the offers the open level-up overlay presents, the offers the next
  overlay will present when the surface has set them, how many level-ups are
  earned and not yet resolved, and what the open chest overlay reports.
- Every enemy alive: a unique id, its type, its world position, its health and
  maximum health, the unit heading it moves along, its age in seconds since it
  spawned, and the seconds until it can hit the lamplighter again.
- Every projectile: a unique id, the weapon that fired it, its world position,
  its velocity in units per second and its acceleration in units per second
  squared, its collision radius, its seconds left, its remaining pierce, and
  the enemies it has hit with the seconds until each may be hit again.
- Every zone, an effect that is not a projectile: a unique id, the weapon, its
  kind (`puddle`, `lantern`, `aura`, `slash`, `strike`, or `burst`), its world
  position, its radius, the rectangle of a slash, its seconds left or that it
  never expires, and the enemies it has hit with the seconds until each may be
  hit again.
- Every gem: a unique id, its tier, its world position, and whether it is
  attracted. Every pickup: a unique id, its kind (`chest`, `bread`, or
  `draft`), and its world position.
- The spawn director: the seconds until the next window spawn, whether the
  director is on, and the run-clock times of the scripted events that have
  fired this run.
- The next id to assign, one counter shared by enemies, projectiles, zones,
  gems, and pickups and never reused within a run.
- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds its `dt` to whatever
  the screen. The snapshot reports it as `simTime`.
- Whether audio is muted, and the state of the seeded random generator every
  random draw comes from.

Off the run, on `title` and `howto`, the run's fields hold their idle values:
tick `0`, level `1`, no experience, no kills, the lamplighter at the world
origin facing right with `BASE_MAX_HP` (`100`) health, no weapons, no passives,
nothing alive, nothing dropped, no offers, no level-ups earned, no chest
result, the spawn timer at `0`, the director on, no events fired, and the next
id `0`. The `fallen` and `dawn` screens keep the run that just ended, since
they report its time, level, and kills.
{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The six id and tier aliases `src/constants.ts` already exports are
imported and re-exported rather than declared a second time. The state is a
value the engine holds and every frame replaces, so every field and every array
is declared `readonly`: a frame builds the next state from the current one
rather than writing into it, and every reader is handed the state as
`DeepReadonly<WickState>` in any case.

```ts
import type {
  EnemyId,
  GemTier,
  OfferId,
  PassiveId,
  PickupKind,
  WeaponId,
} from "./constants";

export type { EnemyId, GemTier, OfferId, PassiveId, PickupKind, WeaponId };

export type Screen =
  | "title"
  | "howto"
  | "playing"
  | "levelup"
  | "chest"
  | "paused"
  | "fallen"
  | "dawn";

export type Facing = "left" | "right";

export type ZoneKind =
  | "puddle"
  | "lantern"
  | "aura"
  | "slash"
  | "strike"
  | "burst";

export type ChestResult =
  | { readonly kind: "evolve"; readonly weapon: WeaponId }
  | {
      readonly kind: "level";
      readonly item: WeaponId | PassiveId;
      readonly level: number;
    }
  | { readonly kind: "heal" };

export interface PlayerState {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly facing: Facing;
  readonly hp: number;
}

export interface WeaponSlot {
  readonly id: WeaponId;
  readonly level: number;
  readonly cooldown: number;
}

export interface PassiveSlot {
  readonly id: PassiveId;
  readonly level: number;
}

export interface EnemyState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly type: EnemyId;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly hp: number;
  readonly maxHp: number;
  readonly heading: { readonly x: number; readonly y: number };
  readonly age: number;
  readonly contactCooldown: number;
}

export interface EnemyHit {
  readonly enemy: number;
  readonly cooldown: number;
}

export interface ProjectileState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly weapon: WeaponId;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly vx: number;
  readonly vy: number;
  readonly ax: number;
  readonly ay: number;
  readonly radius: number;
  readonly ttl: number;
  readonly pierce: number;
  readonly hits: readonly EnemyHit[];
}

export interface ZoneState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly weapon: WeaponId;
  readonly kind: ZoneKind;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly radius: number;
  readonly width?: number;
  readonly height?: number;
  readonly ttl: number | null;
  readonly hits: readonly EnemyHit[];
}

export interface GemState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly tier: GemTier;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly attracted: boolean;
}

export interface PickupState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly kind: PickupKind;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
}

export interface RunState {
  readonly tick: number;
  readonly level: number;
  readonly xp: number;
  readonly kills: number;
  readonly player: PlayerState;
  readonly weapons: readonly WeaponSlot[];
  readonly passives: readonly PassiveSlot[];
  readonly enemies: readonly EnemyState[];
  readonly projectiles: readonly ProjectileState[];
  readonly zones: readonly ZoneState[];
  readonly gems: readonly GemState[];
  readonly pickups: readonly PickupState[];
  readonly offers: readonly OfferId[];
  readonly nextOffers: readonly OfferId[] | null;
  readonly pendingLevelUps: number;
  readonly chestResult: ChestResult | null;
  readonly spawnTimer: number;
  readonly autoSpawn: boolean;
  readonly firedEvents: readonly number[];
  readonly nextId: number;
}

export interface WickState {
  readonly screen: Screen;
  readonly menuIndex: number;
  readonly run: RunState;
  readonly accumulator: number;
  readonly simTime: number;
  readonly muted: boolean;
  readonly rngState: number;
}
```

## The type aliases

`Screen` is the top-level state machine, and `screen` names the screen the game
is showing. `Facing` is the side the lamplighter faces and the direction Taper,
Pin, and their evolutions fire in. `WeaponId` names the ten base weapons and the
six evolutions, the members of `BASE_WEAPON_IDS` and `EVOLUTION_IDS`;
`PassiveId` the ten passives of `PASSIVE_IDS`; `OfferId` everything a level-up
overlay can present, which is every weapon, every passive, and `LAMP_OIL_ID`
(`"lamp-oil"`); `EnemyId` the thirteen enemies of `ENEMY_IDS`; `GemTier` the
three tiers of `GEM_TIERS`; and `PickupKind` the three pickups of
`PICKUP_KINDS`. `ZoneKind` names the six kinds of zone, under `ZoneState`
below.

## `PlayerState`

The lamplighter.

- `x`, `y` — the center of the lamplighter's collision circle, of radius
  `PLAYER_RADIUS` (`12`), in world units. The world origin is where the run
  began, so a fresh run holds `0, 0`.
- `facing` — `"right"` when a run begins, and afterwards the direction of the
  most recent non-zero horizontal input.
- `hp` — the current health, a real number at most `maxHp`; a hit may take it
  below `0`, which ends the run. Maximum health
  is derived from the held Tallow level as `BASE_MAX_HP` (`100`) plus
  `TALLOW_HP_PER_LEVEL` (`15`) per level, so it is read off `passives` rather
  than stored.

## `WeaponSlot` and `PassiveSlot`

One held weapon and one held passive. `weapons` and `passives` list the slots
in slot order: an item joins the first free slot of its kind, an evolution
replaces its base weapon in the same slot, and a run begins with Taper at level
`1` in the first weapon slot and no passives.

- `id` — the item held in the slot, base or evolved for a weapon.
- `level` — from `1` to `MAX_WEAPON_LEVEL` (`8`) for a base weapon and `1` for
  an evolved one; from `1` to the passive's `maxLevel` in `PASSIVES` for a
  passive.
- `cooldown` — the weapon's cooldown timer, a timer as `specs/world.md`
  defines one: the seconds until the weapon next fires, counting down while a
  run ticks. It is `0` on acquisition and on evolution, so a weapon fires on
  the first `playing` tick it is held. For Halo and Corona it is the seconds to
  the next pulse; for Lantern it is set on firing to the set's `duration` plus
  the current cooldown, so it counts the set's life and the gap after it
  together; and Chandelier, which never fires again, holds `0`.

## `EnemyState`

One enemy alive, in `enemies`.

- `id` — unique for the run, assigned from `nextId` when the enemy spawns.
- `type` — the enemy's row in `ENEMIES`.
- `x`, `y` — the center of the enemy's collision circle, of the radius its row
  gives. For a weaving enemy this is the anchor plus the perpendicular offset,
  so the anchor is derived by subtracting the offset that `heading` and `age`
  give.
- `hp`, `maxHp` — real numbers. `maxHp` is set once, at spawn: the row's `hp`
  times `1 + HP_SCALE_PER_MINUTE` (`0.15`) `× floor(time / 60)` for a common
  enemy, and the row's `hp` unscaled for an elite and for the Dark. `hp` begins
  at `maxHp`.
- `heading` — the unit vector the enemy moves along. A chasing and a weaving
  enemy recompute it toward the lamplighter's center on every tick before
  moving; a drifting enemy keeps the heading it spawned with.
- `age` — seconds since the enemy spawned, growing by `TICK_DT` per tick.
- `contactCooldown` — a timer as `specs/world.md` defines one, the seconds
  until the enemy can hit the lamplighter again: `0` when it can,
  `CONTACT_COOLDOWN` (`0.5`) the tick it hits, counting down every tick whether
  or not it is in contact.

## `ProjectileState`

One projectile in flight, in `projectiles`: an Ember, Beacon, Pin, or Hail
bolt, a Shard, or a Sconce.

- `id` — unique for the run, assigned from `nextId`.
- `weapon` — the weapon that fired it.
- `x`, `y` — the center of its collision circle.
- `vx`, `vy` — its velocity, in units per second.
- `ax`, `ay` — its acceleration, in units per second squared, added to the
  velocity every tick before the position integrates. Every projectile holds
  `0, 0` except a Sconce, whose acceleration is `SCONCE_DECEL` (`600`) along the
  opposite of its launch direction.
- `radius` — its collision radius, the level table's value times the area
  multiplier in force when it was fired.
- `ttl` — the seconds it has left, a timer as `specs/world.md` defines one,
  set to its `duration` when it is fired. The projectile is removed on the
  tick `ttl` is due.
- `pierce` — how many more enemies it may pass through after a hit. It falls
  by one per enemy hit, a projectile at `0` is removed by the hit that would
  take it lower, and `INFINITE_PIERCE` (`-1`) marks a projectile hits never
  lower or remove.
- `hits` — the enemies it has hit and may not hit again yet, under `EnemyHit`.

## `EnemyHit`

One entry of a projectile's or a zone's `hits`.

- `enemy` — the id of the enemy hit.
- `cooldown` — the seconds until this projectile or zone may hit that enemy
  again, a timer as `specs/world.md` defines one; an entry that is due is
  dropped. A Shard, a Sconce, and a lantern set it to their weapon's re-hit
  interval. A projectile with finite pierce sets it to its own remaining `ttl`,
  so it hits each enemy at most once in its life.

## `ZoneState`

One effect that is not a projectile, in `zones`.

- `id` — unique for the run, assigned from `nextId`.
- `weapon` — the weapon that produced it.
- `kind` — `puddle` for an Oil Splash or Blaze puddle, `lantern` for one
  orbiting lantern of Lantern or Chandelier, `aura` for the Halo or Corona
  aura, `slash` for a Taper or Pyre slash, `strike` for a Spark strike, and
  `burst` for a Flare burst.
- `x`, `y` — the center of the circle, and for a slash the center of the
  rectangle. A lantern and an aura are placed relative to the lamplighter every
  tick, so their positions follow the lamplighter.
- `radius` — the circle's radius after the area multiplier, and `0` for a
  slash. It is fixed when the zone is created, except on an aura and a
  Chandelier lantern, where it is recomputed on every tick from the level and
  the area multiplier in force on that tick.
- `width`, `height` — the full extent of a slash's rectangle, after the area
  multiplier. They are present on a slash and absent on every other kind.
- `ttl` — the seconds the zone has left, a timer as `specs/world.md` defines
  one, and `null` for a zone that never expires: an aura, and a Chandelier
  lantern. A zone is removed on the tick `ttl` is due. A slash holds
  `SLASH_FLASH` (`0.1`), a strike `SPARK_FLASH` (`0.2`), and a burst
  `FLARE_FLASH` (`0.4`), each drawn for that long and dealing its damage on the
  tick it appears alone; a Lantern lantern holds the set's `duration` and a
  puddle its `duration`.
- `hits` — for a lantern, the enemies it may not hit again yet, under
  `EnemyHit`. Every other kind holds an empty list, since a puddle and an aura
  pulse over every enemy they overlap and a slash, a strike, and a burst hit on
  the tick they appear.

A lantern's angle around the lamplighter is derived from its position relative
to the lamplighter, so the next tick's position is that angle advanced by
`LANTERN_ANGULAR_SPEED` (`180`) `× TICK_DT` degrees and placed at the orbit
radius around the lamplighter's new position. A Chandelier lantern's orbit
radius is recomputed every tick, and when Chandelier's amount changes its
lanterns are replaced by new zones, as `specs/evolutions.md` states.

## `GemState` and `PickupState`

One gem and one pickup lying on the field, in `gems` and `pickups`.

- `id` — unique for the run, assigned from `nextId`.
- `tier` — the gem's tier, worth `GEM_VALUES[tier]` experience before the
  experience multiplier.
- `kind` — the pickup's kind.
- `x`, `y` — its center in world units.
- `attracted` — whether the gem is flying to the lamplighter. It becomes
  `true` on the tick the gem is within the pickup radius, or on the tick a
  draft is collected, and stays `true`.

## `ChestResult`

What the open chest overlay reports. `evolve` carries the id of the weapon the
chest produced, `level` carries the item that gained a level and the level it
became, and `heal` reports the `CHEST_HEAL` (`30`) fallback.

## `RunState`

The run in progress or just ended.

- `tick` — ticks since the run started. The run clock is `tick / TICK_HZ`
  (`60`) seconds, and the run ends at dawn on the tick the clock reaches
  `DAWN_TIME` (`600`).
- `level` — the lamplighter's level, starting at `1`.
- `xp` — the experience toward the next level, a real number below
  `xpToNext(level)`, which is `XP_BASE` (`5`) `+ XP_STEP` (`10`)
  `× (level − 1)`.
- `kills` — how many enemies have died this run.
- `player` — the lamplighter.
- `weapons`, `passives` — the loadout, in slot order.
- `enemies`, `projectiles`, `zones`, `gems`, `pickups` — everything alive or
  lying on the field, each list ascending by id.
- `offers` — the offers the open level-up overlay presents, top to bottom,
  and empty on every other screen.
- `nextOffers` — the offers the next level-up overlay will present, when the
  debug surface has set them, and `null` otherwise. Opening an overlay consumes
  it.
- `pendingLevelUps` — how many level-ups have been earned and not yet resolved
  by a choice, the one the open overlay presents included. It is at least `1`
  whenever `screen` is `levelup`.
- `chestResult` — what the open chest overlay reports, and `null` on every
  other screen.
- `spawnTimer` — the seconds until the director's next window spawn. It is `0`
  when a run starts and on every window change.
- `autoSpawn` — whether the director, the window spawns and the scripted
  events, is running.
- `firedEvents` — the run-clock times, in seconds, of the scripted events that
  have fired this run, ascending.
- `nextId` — the next id to assign, one counter shared by enemies,
  projectiles, zones, gems, and pickups. It is `0` when a run starts and is
  never reused within a run.

## The idle run

`run` holds the values below whenever `screen` is `title` or `howto`:
`initialize` and `reset` build them, and leaving a run for the title restores
them. The `fallen` and `dawn` screens keep the run that just ended, since they
report its time, level, and kills.

| Field | Idle value |
| --- | --- |
| `tick`, `level`, `xp`, `kills` | `0`, `1`, `0`, `0` |
| `player` | `{ x: 0, y: 0, facing: "right", hp: BASE_MAX_HP }` |
| `weapons`, `passives` | empty |
| `enemies`, `projectiles`, `zones`, `gems`, `pickups` | empty |
| `offers` | empty |
| `nextOffers` | `null` |
| `pendingLevelUps` | `0` |
| `chestResult` | `null` |
| `spawnTimer` | `0` |
| `autoSpawn` | `true` |
| `firedEvents` | empty |
| `nextId` | `0` |

A fresh run is the idle run with Taper at level `1` and cooldown `0` in the
first weapon slot.

## `WickState`

The whole of Wick's state. Every field is present from the moment `initialize`
returns, and every one is plain data: numbers, strings, booleans, `null`, and
containers of them, so a scenario is posed by building a state that differs in
the fields posed and read back by reading them.

- `screen` — the screen currently shown.
- `menuIndex` — the highlighted item on whichever vertical menu `screen` is
  showing. It is `0` on entering every screen, and on a screen with no menu it
  stays `0`.
- `run` — the run in progress or just ended, and the idle run on `title` and
  `howto`.
- `accumulator` — the frame time waiting for the next whole tick, in seconds,
  at least `0` and below `TICK_DT`. It grows on `playing` alone: the delta
  time left unconsumed when the screen leaves `playing` is discarded, so it
  holds `0` on every other screen.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every `update` adds its
  `dt`, whatever the screen, the menus and the overlays included.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the engine's mute bit, which every
  `update` carries into the state it returns. The engine owns muting, and this
  field is what the debug surface reports.
- `rngState` — the state of the game's one seeded random generator. A `reset`
  with a seed sets it, and every random draw advances it: a spawn's angle and
  type, an offer draw, a puddle's landing point, a strike's target, a chest's
  fallback item, a swarm's direction, and the bread and draft draws.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by
  the time a frame can observe it. `ZoneState.width` and `height` are the one
  optional pair, present on a slash alone.
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. A
  `reset` restores the declared fields to their title-screen values, so those
  fields are the whole of the authoritative state.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one
  frame to the next lives in it. Loaded images and decoded audio are the one
  exception: `initialize` may hold them in a module-level table `render` reads,
  since they are not game state.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<WickState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of
  the debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new `WickState`
  built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and `snapshot`
  return no state. The type is what guarantees that rendering changes nothing
  and that nothing but a transition advances the game.
{{/if}}
